
<p>Jason Zweig, back in 2018:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p><em><a href="http://jasonzweig.com/on-fathers-day/">My father</a>, who died in 1981, was an inexhaustible font of
wisdom and wit. I don’t know when he told me this particular
three-part rule, but I’ve never forgotten it. I tweeted it
three years ago, but people keep asking for it in one place, so
here it is.</em></p>

<p>There are three ways to make a living:</p>

<ol>
<li><p>Lie to people who want to be lied to, and you’ll get rich.</p></li>
<li><p>Tell the truth to those who want the truth, and you’ll make a
living.</p></li>
<li><p>Tell the truth to those who want to be lied to, and you’ll go
broke.</p></li>
</ol>

<p><em>The rest is commentary.</em></p>
</blockquote>

<p>Pairs well with Om Malik’s remarkable line about the success of “the grifters and the hucksters and the influencers selling impossible things” in his “<a href="https://om.co/2026/05/25/we-are-living-in-pinocchios-world/">We Are Living in Pinocchio’s World</a>” essay that I <a href="https://daringfireball.net/linked/2026/06/01/pinocchios-world">linked to yesterday</a>.</p>

